      {
        "id": "p5-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the arithmetic mean of $8$ and $18$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$13$",
          "worked": "$$\\frac{8+18}{2}=13.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the geometric mean of $8$ and $18$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$12$",
          "worked": "$$\\sqrt{8\\times18}=\\sqrt{144}=12.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\frac{x+y}{2}\\ge\\sqrt{xy}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{x+y}{2}\\ge\\sqrt{xy}$.\n\nThe difference is $\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})^2}{2}\\ge0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+y}{2}-\\sqrt{xy}&=\\frac{x-2\\sqrt{xy}+y}{2}\\\\\\frac{x+y}{2}-\\sqrt{xy}&=\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^2-2\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}+(\\sqrt{y})^2}{2}\\\\\\frac{x+y}{2}-\\sqrt{xy}&=\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})^2}{2}\\\\\\frac{x+y}{2}-\\sqrt{xy}&\\ge0\\end{align*}$$\nThe numerator is non-negative and the denominator is positive. Hence $\\frac{x+y}{2}\\ge\\sqrt{xy}$, with equality when $x=y$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q7-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "A rectangle has sides of length $x$ and $y$. A square is drawn that has the same perimeter as the rectangle. Show that the area of the rectangle is less than or equal to the area of the square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$A_{\\text{rectangle}}=xy\\le\\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right)^2=A_{\\text{square}}$.\n\nThe square has side length $\\frac{x+y}{2}$; square the non-negative quantities in part (c).",
          "worked": "The rectangle has perimeter $2x+2y$ and area $xy$. The square therefore has side length $\\frac{2x+2y}{4}=\\frac{x+y}{2}$.\n\nBy part (c), $\\frac{x+y}{2}\\ge\\sqrt{xy}$. Both sides are non-negative, so squaring preserves the inequality:\n$$\\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right)^2\\ge xy.$$\nThus the area of the square is at least the area of the rectangle, with equality when $x=y$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      }
    ]
  },
